module

public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Spaces.ClosedSubspaceIdealBijection

/-!
# The zero scheme of a section is maximal among the closed subspaces killing it

Let `X` be an algebraic space over a scheme `S` contained in a big fppf site `B`, let `L` be an
`𝒪_X`-module and let `s` be a global section of `L`.  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.zeroScheme` of
Stacks 0845 is defined, in
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Spaces/ZeroScheme.lean`, as the intersection of the closed
subspaces whose defining ideal is contained in the ideal generated by the local evaluations of
`s`.  This file relates that intersection to the defining ideals on both sides.

Stacks 0846 part (1) says that `Z(s)` is the *maximal* closed subspace on which `s` pulls back
to zero.  Chartwise, "`s` pulls back to zero on `Z`" is the inclusion of the evaluation ideal of
`s` into the defining ideal of `Z`, so part (1) has two halves: every closed subspace killing
`s` is contained in `Z(s)`, and `Z(s)` itself kills `s`.  **The first half is proved here,
unconditionally.** The second is not, and cannot be with what the library has: see the
implementation notes.  Accordingly **no Stacks 0846 tag is placed here**, and no part of
Stacks 0846 is claimed.

## Main definitions

* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.sectionEvaluationIdealAt` is the ideal of Stacks 0845 on an étale
  chart `U` of `X`: the ideal generated by the values of the linear functionals on `L(U)` at the
  section `s`, i.e. the image on `U` of the map `s : L^{⊗ -1} ⟶ 𝒪_X` when `L` is invertible.
  It is definitionally the ideal appearing in the definition of
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.zeroSchemeSubfunctor`, which
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.zeroSchemeSubfunctor_eq_sInf` records.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.le_zeroSchemeSubfunctor` is the **maximality half of Stacks 0846
  (1)**: a closed subspace whose defining ideal contains the evaluation ideal of `s` on every
  affine étale chart is contained in `Z(s)`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.zeroSchemeSubfunctor_le` is the complementary bound, immediate from
  the definition: `Z(s)` is contained in every closed subspace whose defining ideal is contained
  in the evaluation ideal of `s`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.zeroSchemeSubfunctor_eq` combines the two: **if some closed subspace
  of `X` has the evaluation ideal of `s` as its defining ideal, then `Z(s)` is that subspace.**
  This is the exact reduction of Stacks 0846 to the one missing input.

## Implementation notes

The maximality half uses no quasi-coherence and no pullback of modules.  Its content is the
injectivity half of Stacks 03MB, `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.le_of_forall_definingIdealAt_le`: if
`W` ranges over the closed subspaces whose defining ideal is contained in the evaluation ideal
of `s`, and the defining ideal of `Z` contains that evaluation ideal, then the defining ideal of
`W` is contained in that of `Z` on every affine chart, whence `Z ⊆ W`; so `Z` is below the
intersection.  In particular the obstruction to Stacks 0846 is *not* the absence of a pullback
functor on modules over an algebraic space — that functor does not enter the maximality half at
all, and `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.exists_comp_eq_iff_forall_ker_le` states the factorization
criterion of Stacks 03MB without it.

What is missing is the *surjectivity* half of Stacks 03MB: that a quasi-coherent ideal of `𝒪_X`
is the defining ideal of some closed subspace.  Without it the family indexing the intersection
is not known to contain a subspace whose defining ideal is the evaluation ideal, and then
nothing forces `Z(s)` to kill `s` — the intersection of an empty family is `X` itself, on which
`s` need not vanish.  The two obstructions to that half are recorded in the section "What is not
proved" of `StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Spaces/ClosedSubspaceIdealBijection.lean`; both run
through the identification of quasi-coherent modules on the small étale site of a scheme with
quasi-coherent modules on the scheme, Stacks 03DX (2) and 03LX, which is carried as the
hypothesis `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.IsCartesianOnAffineCharts` and is blocked on Stacks 03DW.

The scheme-level statement is not the obstruction: `AlgebraicGeometry.zeroScheme` in
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Divisors/ZeroSchemeData.lean` constructs the zero scheme of a
section of an invertible module on a scheme as a quasi-coherent ideal sheaf and identifies its
ideal on every affine open, which is Stacks 02OQ.  So a closed subspace with the right defining
ideal exists over each étale scheme chart of `X`; what is not available is the comparison that
puts those chartwise ideals back on the small étale site of `X`.

No invertibility hypothesis on `L` appears, following
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.zeroSchemeSubfunctor_isClosedSubspace`, which is also stated for a
section of an arbitrary `𝒪_X`-module.  For a general `L` the evaluation ideal is still the image
of the evaluation map, so the statements below are the ones Stacks 0846 (1) makes once `L` is
invertible and are not weakened by dropping the hypothesis.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0845](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0845)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0846](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0846)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 03MB](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03MB)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 02OQ](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02OQ)
-/

@[expose] public section

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits Opposite

namespace AlgebraicGeometry.Space

universe u

variable (B : BigFppfSite.{u}) (S : Scheme.{u}) (hS : B.Contains S)

/-- **The ideal of Stacks 0845 on an étale chart.** For a section `s` of an `𝒪_X`-module `L` and
an étale chart `U` of `X`, the ideal of `𝒪_X(U)` generated by the values `l (s|_U)` of the
linear functionals `l` on `L(U)`.  When `L` is invertible this is the image on `U` of the map
`s : L^{⊗ -1} ⟶ 𝒪_X` which Stacks 0845 uses to cut out the zero scheme. -/
noncomputable def sectionEvaluationIdealAt (X : AlgebraicSpaces B S hS)
    (L : Modules.{u, u} B S hS X.1 X.2) (s : L.sections)
    (U : SmallEtale B S hS X.1) :
    Ideal (ToType ((structureRingSheaf B S hS X).obj.obj (op U))) :=
  Ideal.span (Set.range (fun l : Module.Dual
    ((Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf
      (smallEtaleRingedSite B S hS X.1 X.2).O).obj.obj (op U))
    (L.val.obj (op U)) => l (s.eval (op U))))

/-- The zero scheme of Stacks 0845 is the intersection of the closed subspaces whose defining
ideal is contained in the evaluation ideal of the section on every affine étale chart.  This is
the definition of `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.zeroSchemeSubfunctor` restated through
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.sectionEvaluationIdealAt`. -/
theorem zeroSchemeSubfunctor_eq_sInf (X : AlgebraicSpaces B S hS)
    (L : Modules.{u, u} B S hS X.1 X.2) (s : L.sections) :
    zeroSchemeSubfunctor B S hS X L s =
      sInf {Z | ∃ hZ : IsClosedSubspace B S hS Z,
        ∀ U : SmallEtale B S hS X.1,
          IsAffine ((SmallEtale.toSchemes B S hS X.1).obj U).1 →
          definingIdealAt B S hS X ⟨Z, hZ⟩ (op U) ≤
            sectionEvaluationIdealAt B S hS X L s U} :=
  rfl

/-- **The zero scheme is contained in every closed subspace whose defining ideal is contained in
the evaluation ideal of the section.**  Such a subspace is a member of the family being
intersected. -/
theorem zeroSchemeSubfunctor_le (X : AlgebraicSpaces B S hS)
    (L : Modules.{u, u} B S hS X.1 X.2) (s : L.sections)
    (Z : ClosedSubspaces B S hS X.1)
    (h : ∀ U : SmallEtale B S hS X.1,
      IsAffine ((SmallEtale.toSchemes B S hS X.1).obj U).1 →
      definingIdealAt B S hS X Z (op U) ≤ sectionEvaluationIdealAt B S hS X L s U) :
    zeroSchemeSubfunctor B S hS X L s ≤ Z.1 :=
  sInf_le ⟨Z.2, h⟩

/-- **The maximality half of Stacks 0846 (1).**  A closed subspace of `X` whose defining ideal
contains the evaluation ideal of the section on every affine étale chart — chartwise, a closed
subspace on which the section pulls back to zero — is contained in the zero scheme of that
section.

Every member `W` of the family intersected by `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.zeroSchemeSubfunctor` has
defining ideal contained in the evaluation ideal, hence in that of `Z`, so `Z ⊆ W` by the
injectivity half of Stacks 03MB. -/
theorem le_zeroSchemeSubfunctor (X : AlgebraicSpaces B S hS)
    (L : Modules.{u, u} B S hS X.1 X.2) (s : L.sections)
    (Z : ClosedSubspaces B S hS X.1)
    (h : ∀ U : SmallEtale B S hS X.1,
      IsAffine ((SmallEtale.toSchemes B S hS X.1).obj U).1 →
      sectionEvaluationIdealAt B S hS X L s U ≤ definingIdealAt B S hS X Z (op U)) :
    Z.1 ≤ zeroSchemeSubfunctor B S hS X L s := by
  refine le_sInf ?_
  rintro W ⟨hW, hWle⟩
  exact le_of_forall_definingIdealAt_le B S hS X Z ⟨W, hW⟩
    fun U hU => (hWle U hU).trans (h U hU)

/-- **Stacks 0846 reduced to the surjectivity of Stacks 03MB.**  If a closed subspace of `X` has
the evaluation ideal of the section as its defining ideal on every affine étale chart, then the
zero scheme of the section is that subspace.

The hypothesis is exactly what the missing half of Stacks 03MB would supply, and with it every
assertion of Stacks 0846 about `Z(s)` becomes an assertion about a closed subspace with a
prescribed defining ideal. -/
theorem zeroSchemeSubfunctor_eq (X : AlgebraicSpaces B S hS)
    (L : Modules.{u, u} B S hS X.1 X.2) (s : L.sections)
    (Z : ClosedSubspaces B S hS X.1)
    (h : ∀ U : SmallEtale B S hS X.1,
      IsAffine ((SmallEtale.toSchemes B S hS X.1).obj U).1 →
      definingIdealAt B S hS X Z (op U) = sectionEvaluationIdealAt B S hS X L s U) :
    zeroSchemeSubfunctor B S hS X L s = Z.1 :=
  le_antisymm (zeroSchemeSubfunctor_le B S hS X L s Z fun U hU => (h U hU).le)
    (le_zeroSchemeSubfunctor B S hS X L s Z fun U hU => (h U hU).ge)

end AlgebraicGeometry.Space

end
